-## Correctness Properties
-
-A property is a characteristic or behavior that should hold true across all valid executions of a system—essentially, a formal statement about what the system should do. Properties serve as the bridge between human-readable specifications and machine-verifiable correctness guarantees.
-
-### Property Reflection
-
-After analyzing all acceptance criteria, I identified the following redundancies:
-
-**Redundancy Analysis**:
-1. Properties 6.1, 6.2, 6.3, 6.4 all test output completeness - can be combined into one comprehensive property
-2. Properties 2.3 and 2.4 both test layer height calculation - can be combined into one property covering both stackable and non-stackable cases
-3. Properties 7.1, 7.2, 7.5 all test API compatibility - can be combined into one comprehensive property
-4. Property 5.4 (weight constraints) is already validated by existing compliance checks, but should be explicit
-5. Property 3.4 (dimension validation) is implied by successful placement, but should be explicit for clarity
-
-**Consolidation Decisions**:
-- Combine output completeness properties into Property 12
-- Combine layer height calculation properties into Property 4
-- Combine API compatibility properties into Property 14
-- Keep weight and dimension validation as explicit properties for safety-critical checks
-
-### Property 1: Grid Size GCD Calculation
-
-For any set of cargo items with dimensions, the calculated grid size should be the greatest common divisor (GCD) of all unique dimension values, clamped to the range [10mm, 50mm].
-
-**Validates: Requirements 1.1, 1.3**
-
-### Property 2: Grid Alignment
-
-For any placed item in the packing plan, the x and y coordinates must be exact multiples of the calculated grid size.
-
-**Validates: Requirements 1.4**
-
-### Property 3: Layer Height Optimization
-
-For any layer in the packing plan, the unused vertical space (layer.maxHeight - actual_max_height_used) must be less than or equal to 100mm.
-
-**Validates: Requirements 2.2**
-
-### Property 4: Layer Height Calculation
-
-For any layer containing items, the layer height must equal the maximum stacked height of items in that layer (considering stackable items as item_height × stack_count) plus clearance (≤100mm).
-
-**Validates: Requirements 2.3, 2.4**
-
-### Property 5: Rotation Evaluation Completeness
-
-For any item being placed, the rotation selection algorithm must evaluate all 6 possible orientations (LWH, WLH, LHW, HWL, WHL, HLW) before selecting the optimal rotation.
-
-**Validates: Requirements 3.1**
-
-### Property 6: Footprint Area Minimization
-
-For any placed item, the selected rotation must have the minimum footprint area (length × width) among all valid rotations that satisfy height and container dimension constraints.
-
-**Validates: Requirements 3.2**
-
-### Property 7: Rotation Tiebreaker
-
-For any placed item where multiple valid rotations have footprint areas within 5% of each other, the selected rotation must be the one with the smallest height.
-
-**Validates: Requirements 3.3**
-
-### Property 8: Container Dimension Validation
-
-For any placed item, all three dimensions (length, width, height) in the selected rotation must be less than or equal to the corresponding container dimensions (innerLength, innerWidth, innerHeight).
-
-**Validates: Requirements 3.4**
-
-### Property 9: Layer Filling Priority
-
-For any two items placed in different layers, if item A is placed in a lower layer than item B, then item A must have been placed before or during the time when the lower layer was being filled (lower layers filled first).
-
-**Validates: Requirements 4.1**
-
-### Property 10: Stackable Item Layer Assignment
-
-For any two stackable items with different stack limits, the item with the higher stack limit should appear in a layer with a lower or equal z-start position (higher stack limit items in lower layers).
-
-**Validates: Requirements 4.2**
-
-### Property 11: Height Grouping Within Layers
-
-For any layer containing multiple items, the variance in item heights within that layer should be minimized compared to random assignment (items with similar heights grouped together).
-
-**Validates: Requirements 4.4**
-
-### Property 12: Non-Stackable Item Placement
-
-For any non-stackable item in the packing plan, there must be no other items placed directly above it (no item with position.z >= non_stackable_item.position.z + non_stackable_item.height and overlapping x,y footprint).
-
-**Validates: Requirements 4.5**
-
-### Property 13: Performance Constraint
-
-For any cargo set with up to 500 items, the packing computation must complete within 10 seconds.
-
-**Validates: Requirements 5.3, 8.2**
-
-### Property 14: Weight Constraint Compliance
-
-For any packing plan, the total weight of all placed items must be less than or equal to the container's maximum weight capacity.
-
-**Validates: Requirements 5.4**
-
-### Property 15: Stacking Constraint Compliance
-
-For any placed item at position (x, y, z), the number of items of the same type stacked at that (x, y) location must not exceed the item's maxStackLayers property.
-
-**Validates: Requirements 5.5**
-
-### Property 16: Visualization Data Completeness
-
-For any packing plan, the visualization data must include: (1) position coordinates (x, y, z) for every placed item, (2) dimension data (length, width, height) for every placed item, (3) layer boundary data with height ranges, and (4) summary statistics (space utilization %, loading rate %, total weight).
-
-**Validates: Requirements 6.1, 6.2, 6.3, 6.4**
-
-### Property 17: Visualization Data Format
-
-For any packing plan, the visualization data must be valid JSON and contain the required structure: container object with dimensions, cargo array with position and dimension objects, centerOfGravity object, and loadDistribution object.
-
-**Validates: Requirements 6.5**
-
-### Property 18: API Compatibility
-
-The LayeredPackingEngine must accept input in the format (cargoList: Array, containerType: Object) and return output with the structure { loadedCargo: Array, unloadedCargo: Array, spaceUtilization: Number, totalWeight: Number, visualizationData: Object, compliance: Object }.
-
-**Validates: Requirements 7.1, 7.2, 7.5**
-
-### Property 19: Deterministic Behavior
-
-For any cargo set and container type, running the packing algorithm multiple times with the same input must produce identical output (same item positions, same rotations, same order).
-
-**Validates: Requirements 8.3**
-
-### Property 20: Debug Logging
-
-For any packing execution, the algorithm must log intermediate steps including: grid size calculation, layer creation with heights, item placement attempts, and rotation selections.
-
-**Validates: Requirements 8.4**
-
