-# Bugfix Requirements Document
-
-## Introduction
-
-The Realistic4DPackingEngine contains three critical logic inversions that violate LIFO (Last-In-First-Out) unloading requirements and render the cargo classification system non-functional. Visual evidence shows all cargo piling up at the container door (X=0 plane, red wireframe) with the back wall completely empty, creating a physically impossible loading scenario. Additionally, all items are incorrectly classified as "Heavy" or "Fragile" with no "Normal" items appearing, and batch separation is not enforced spatially.
-
-These bugs affect the core packing algorithm in `backend/services/Realistic4DPackingEngine.js` and break the fundamental promise of the system: proper LIFO-compliant loading with accurate cargo classification.
-
-## Bug Analysis
-
-### Current Behavior (Defect)
-
-**Bug 1: Loading Direction Inversion**
-
-1.1 WHEN the packing algorithm generates candidate positions THEN the system iterates from X=0 (door) towards X=innerLength (wall), filling the door area first
-
-1.2 WHEN cargo is placed using the current candidate generation THEN all items accumulate at the container door (X=0 plane) leaving the back wall (X=innerLength) completely empty
-
-1.3 WHEN Port 3 items (后卸, last to unload) are packed THEN they are placed near the door instead of deep in the container
-
-1.4 WHEN Port 1 items (先卸, first to unload) are packed THEN they are placed randomly instead of nearest to the door
-
-**Bug 2: Classification Logic Failure**
-
-1.5 WHEN cargo items are classified by `mapPackagingTypeToFrontend()` THEN all items return "Heavy" or "Fragile" with no "Normal" items appearing
-
-1.6 WHEN cargo with varying weights (e.g., 5kg, 10kg, 15kg) is processed THEN the weight-based classification threshold is not applied correctly
-
-1.7 WHEN non-fragile cargo below the weight threshold is classified THEN it fails to return "normal" as the default category
-
-**Bug 3: Batch Sequence Spatial Separation Failure**
-
-1.8 WHEN multiple batches with different portSequence values are packed THEN items from different batches are spatially mixed instead of forming distinct zones
-
-1.9 WHEN Batch 3 (portSequence=3, last to unload) is packed THEN it is not placed at the deepest position (highest X values near wall)
-
-1.10 WHEN Batch 1 (portSequence=1, first to unload) is packed THEN it is not placed nearest to the door (lowest X values)
-
-1.11 WHEN the algorithm processes sorted cargo (by portSequence DESC) THEN it does not enforce batch boundary constraints during candidate generation
-
-### Expected Behavior (Correct)
-
-**Bug 1: Loading Direction Correction**
-
-2.1 WHEN the packing algorithm generates candidate positions THEN the system SHALL iterate from X=innerLength (wall) towards X=0 (door), filling the wall area first
-
-2.2 WHEN cargo is placed using corrected candidate generation THEN items SHALL accumulate at the back wall (X=innerLength) with the door area (X=0) remaining accessible
-
-2.3 WHEN Port 3 items (后卸, last to unload) are packed THEN they SHALL be placed deepest in the container (highest X values near wall)
-
-2.4 WHEN Port 1 items (先卸, first to unload) are packed THEN they SHALL be placed nearest to the door (lowest X values)
-
-**Bug 2: Classification Logic Correction**
-
-2.5 WHEN cargo items are classified by `mapPackagingTypeToFrontend()` THEN the system SHALL return "fragile" for fragile items, "heavy" for items ≥20kg, and "normal" for all other items
-
-2.6 WHEN cargo with varying weights (e.g., 5kg, 10kg, 15kg) is processed THEN items below the weight threshold and not fragile SHALL be classified as "normal"
-
-2.7 WHEN non-fragile cargo below the weight threshold is classified THEN the system SHALL return "normal" as the default category
-
-**Bug 3: Batch Sequence Spatial Separation Correction**
-
-2.8 WHEN multiple batches with different portSequence values are packed THEN items from each batch SHALL form spatially distinct zones along the X-axis
-
-2.9 WHEN Batch 3 (portSequence=3, last to unload) is packed THEN it SHALL occupy the deepest zone (highest X values, near wall)
-
-2.10 WHEN Batch 1 (portSequence=1, first to unload) is packed THEN it SHALL occupy the zone nearest to the door (lowest X values)
-
-2.11 WHEN the algorithm processes sorted cargo (by portSequence DESC) THEN it SHALL enforce batch boundary constraints by tracking the minimum X position for each batch
-
-### Unchanged Behavior (Regression Prevention)
-
-**Packing Algorithm Core Functions**
-
-3.1 WHEN cargo is sorted by portSequence DESC before packing THEN the system SHALL CONTINUE TO sort in descending order (3, 2, 1)
-
-3.2 WHEN the algorithm checks collision detection THEN it SHALL CONTINUE TO use the existing `isColliding()` method without modification
-
-3.3 WHEN the algorithm validates container capacity THEN it SHALL CONTINUE TO respect innerLength, innerWidth, innerHeight boundaries
-
-3.4 WHEN the algorithm calculates space utilization THEN it SHALL CONTINUE TO use the existing volume calculation logic
-
-**Coordinate System and Visualization**
-
-3.5 WHEN the coordinate system is documented THEN it SHALL CONTINUE TO define X=0 as door and X=innerLength as wall
-
-3.6 WHEN 3D visualization renders the container THEN it SHALL CONTINUE TO display the red wireframe at X=0 as the door indicator
-
-3.7 WHEN cargo positions are sent to the frontend THEN it SHALL CONTINUE TO use the same coordinate format {x, y, z, L, W, H}
-
-**Data Flow and API**
-
-3.8 WHEN the packing API receives cargo input THEN it SHALL CONTINUE TO accept the same request format with portSequence, weight, fragile fields
-
-3.9 WHEN the packing result is returned THEN it SHALL CONTINUE TO include packedItems, unpackedItems, and utilization fields
-
-3.10 WHEN cargo metadata is preserved THEN it SHALL CONTINUE TO maintain cargoId, batchId, portSequence, and other original fields
-
-**Safety and Compliance**
-
-3.11 WHEN fragile items are handled THEN the system SHALL CONTINUE TO respect fragile placement rules (no heavy items on top)
-
-3.12 WHEN weight distribution is calculated THEN the system SHALL CONTINUE TO use the existing center-of-gravity logic
-
-3.13 WHEN the algorithm enforces LIFO unloading THEN it SHALL CONTINUE TO prioritize portSequence as the primary sorting criterion
